Based on this, our approach to solve SGBLR is to enumerate the
regions in space formed by H, and in turn, enumerate the realizable
rankings,R. For every ranking 𝜌∈R , we compute the least number
of tuples that need to be allotted a bonus to obtain the ranking 𝜋,
which is equivalent to finding aLongest Common Subsequence (LCS)
between𝜋and𝜌. Since both the inputs to the LCS problems are rank-
ings, it is well-known that the problem is essentially the same as find-
ing aLongest Increasing Subsequence (LIS)in 𝜌where the reference
ranking is𝜋– by renaming the tuples in ascending order based on 𝜋,
i.e., we give the tuple located at 𝜋[0]a new number of 𝑡0and so forth.
The tuples that are not part of the LIS need to be allotted a bonus
so that the linear ranking function 𝑠−1(𝜌)can realize𝜋. Over the set
of rankings𝜌∈R , our algorithm outputs the linear function that had
the longest value for the increasing subsequence. The tuples that are
not part of the subsequence need to be added with a bonus to obtain 𝜋.
We present the pseudo-code for our approach in ERMB (Algorithm 1).
Analysis of ERMB .The analysis of our algorithm relies on two
steps: (i) enumeration of the cells of the arrangement, and (ii) LIS
of the sequences 𝜌. To prove the complexity of the enumeration
step, we rely on Corollary 28.1.2 of [ 54]. The corollary states that the
number of such regions with 𝑛hyperplanes in 𝑑-dimensional space
is bounded byO(𝑛𝑑). We state the Corollary below for easy access.
Proposition 6.[ 54] The maximum combinatorial complexity of
an arrangement of 𝑛hyperplanes in R𝑑isO(𝑛𝑑). Moreover, if the
arrangement is simple, its complexity is indeedΘ(𝑛𝑑).
Observe that each hyperplane 𝐶𝑖,𝑗as shown in Equation 2 has an
intercept of0and hence passes through the origin. Therefore, the
resulting arrangement is a special case of a hyperplane arrangement
known as a central arrangement (see [ 46] for details). In a central
arrangement, the combinatorial complexity of a central arrangement
with𝑚hyperplanes in 𝑑dimensions isO(𝑚𝑑−1)(see [ 46] Chapter 5).

