6.3 Results: Performance and Scalability
Results: RQ1 – Singleton Groups Setting.We experimented
with synthetic two-dimensional data, where the number of tuples
given an additive bonus ( 𝑘) was set to 10% of the total number of
tuples (0.1𝑛). Figure 1a presents the results. As shown, within a time-
out of 1800 seconds, ERMB scales only up to 5,000 tuples, highlighting
its limited practical applicability. This behavior aligns with its theo-
retical complexity of O(𝑛3log𝑛) and motivates the need for practical
MILP-based reformulations.
For the special case of two-dimensional data, the MILP-based ap-
proach MILP Refined scales well, handling up to 75,000 tuples within
450 seconds. However, even MILP Refined fails to scale beyond two
dimensions for singleton groups. Detailed results is shown in the
appendix (Figure 5), after𝑛=5,000MILP Refined times out for𝑑=5.
For the quality analysis of the produced solutions, we compared
the outputs of LocalSearch ,ERMB ,MILP Refined , ordinal regression,
and logistic regression whenever solutions were available. Since
LocalSearch operates under a fixed time budget and relies on ran-
dom sampling, the solution quality depends on the allocated runtime.
As shown in Figure 1c, for two-dimensional data, LocalSearch and
MILP Refined select an almost identical number of tuples to receive an
additive bonus (represented as the best value in the figure) for up to
15,000 tuples. However, as 𝑛increases, the quality of LocalSearch
begins to degrade, even for 𝑑=2. Therefore, we exclude LocalSearch
from the comparison in the remaining research questions. We ob-
serve that ordinal regression and logistic regression also find solu-
tions of low quality, requiring a much larger number of tuples to be
allocated an additive bonus to realize the observed function. This

