Multi-group version of our algorithm: Our idea is to adapt ERMB
(Algorithm 1) to this setting using D𝐺. For every two pairs of tu-
ples𝑡(𝑥)
𝑖,𝑡(𝑦)
𝑗inD𝐺, we introduce a comparison hyperplane in𝑑+𝑔 dimensional space. These␀𝑛(𝑔+1)
2hyperplanes denoted as
H𝐺produce a central arrangement which partitions the space into
(𝑛(𝑔+ 1))2(𝑑+𝑔−1)regions. Similar to ERMB , any weight function in a
region produces the same ranking of the 𝑛(𝑔+ 1)tuples. Thus, we enu-
merate each region and generate the set of all possible rankings R𝐺.
As there are 𝑔+1copies of a tuple, unlike ERMB , this instance of
the sub-problem does not correspond to an instance of the Longest
Increasing Subsequence (LIS) problem, needing us to focus on the
more general Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) problem. Indeed,
given two ordered lists 𝜋and𝜌, an LCS between 𝜋and𝜌gives us
the information about the groups. Thus, as a final step, we perform
abudgetedvariant of LCS between 𝜌∈R and𝜋, such that entries in
𝜋can match any copy of the same tuple in 𝜌, while respecting the
budget constraints. The constrained version of LCS is solved by a
DP with𝑛×𝑛(𝑔+ 1)variables while maintaining a state of 𝑛𝑔. We
present pseudo-code of MGRA (Algorithm 2) for the 𝑔-GBLR problem
and in Algorithm 3 for the budgeted variant of the LCS problem.
Note that, similar to ERMB , our MGRA approach is amenable to
transforming the equations from a 𝑑dimensional space to a (𝑑−1)
dimensional space. Our analysis makes use of this transformation
in producing the runtime complexity.
